Subject: How will UNIX solve the 2038 bug?
Date: 1998/08/27
Message-ID: <6s48de$tu8$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 385343447
Organization: Deja News - The Leader in Internet Discussion
X-Article-Creation-Date: Thu Aug 27 18:26:55 1998 GMT
Newsgroups: comp.sys.sgi,comp.unix.unixware,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.software.year-2000
X-Http-User-Agent: Mozilla/4.04 [en] (WinNT; U)

I realize the year 2038 is a long way off. (If I'm still above ground, I'll
probably be in a home by then). But the year 2000 was once a long way off too
and it is not until now that people are scrambling to prepare for its
associated technical problems.

A similar panic can be avoided in the late 2030's if we start preparing now.
However, there is more that one solution to the 2038 problem. For example,
making UNIX system time an unsigned long integer would defer the problem to
2106. Going to a 64-bit integer could defer it much longer. But, then, would
we still use units of seconds and would year zero still be 1970?

With these things in mind, I ask the following question:

Is there any consensus among those who develop UNIX operating systems as to
which approach will ultimately be adopted to solve the 2038 bug?

