Thomas Anders <anders@hmi.de> writes:
> when mounting filesystems 
>   - from a Digital Unix 4.x server on a Solaris 2.6 client  or
>   - from a Solaris 2.6 server on a Digital Unix 4.0 client
> with NFSv3 over TCP (vers=3, proto=tcp) we notice poor write
> performance (<100 kB/s!) on the client. 

I followup myself with results of my investigations so far.

Who is affected: Solaris 2.6 clients when nfs-mounting from non-Solaris
                 servers.

Results of 'iozone 12' (kB/s):

  Client    Server          v3+tcp    v3+udp      v2+tcp    v2+udp 
  ------    ------          ------    ------      ------    ------
  Sol.2.6   Sol.2.5.1       950/18800 1250/18800  650/18700 315/17500
  Sol.2.5.1 Sol.2.6        1900/13000 2000/ xxxx  270/ 8500 320/ xxxx
  Sol.2.5.1 Dig.Unix4.0D    700/ 9000  600/13000  550/ 5000 590/ 2000
  Sol.2.6   Dig.Unix4.0D     85/ 1000  700/ 1000   85/ 1000 400/  880
  Sol.2.6*  Dig.Unix4.0D     80/  770  640/ 1000  100/17900 390/17800

  Disclaimer: Take these figures as examples rather than fair benchmarks!

  xxxx = widely differing results (probably a network issue)
  * = with 4 NFS tunables suggested by SunService: nfs:nfs_maxasynccount=256,
      nfs:nfs3_max_transfer_size=65536, nfs:nfs_nra=4, nfs:nfs3_nra=4
      (the NFSv2 read tunables seem to help a lot!)

The Solaris 2.6 nfs client problems are pretty obvious:

  1. slow writing with NFSv2/v3 over tcp
  2. slow reading with NFSv3

Problem 1 seems to be fixed in Solaris 2.7 Beta Refresh (I guess I'm not 
allowed to publish figures). Has anyone seen an appropriate nfs bug report 
on Sunsolve that has been "integrated" between 2.6 FCS and s998_15a
without a patch being produced for 2.6?

I have a call with Sun about these issues now. If anyone would like to
contribute (e.g. results with other NFSv3 servers), drop me a line.


Best regards,
Thomas

