The man page should but doesn't say it -- nanosleep() is
in librt. So add -lrt to your build line.

Stay away from the _D9 stuff! It's old cruft from pre-Digital
OSF days and should have been removed ages ago...

Jeff

Niel Markwick wrote:

> I am porting a program from Solaris to Dec Unix and had some problems
> with the nanosleep function.
>
> At first, the program would not link - it claimed nanosleep was an
> undefined symbol.
>
> A search of libc.a revealed that there was no `nanosleep' symbol, but
> there was a nanosleep_d9.
>
> An examination of /usr/include/timers.h reveals that if the macro
> POSIX_4D9 is defined, a further macro is defined, renaming nanosleep to
> nanosleep_d9.  but the POSIX_4D9 macro is never defined, nor is it used
> in any other include file.
>
> My program will now compile and link, but when run, nanosleep() does
> nothing - except return with -1 and set errno to 78 (Function not
