An attempt to upgrade our Digital Unix 3.2C system to version 4.0D failed.
We run this on a DEC 3000 system. According to recent postings in this news
group, the way I tried to follow is:

V3.2C -> V4.0 -> V4.0B -> V4.0D

Already the first step failed. This is wat happened:

1) According to section 2.5 of the installation manual I updated the firmware
using the Firmware update V5.0 CD-ROM.

2) According to section 2.5 of the installation manual I removed all Open3D
kits. (We don't use the other products mentioned in this section.)

3) I booted the system in single-user mode, mounted the Digital Unix V4.0
Operating System CD-ROM as /cdrom and started the upgrade procedure with
/sbin/installupdate -i /cdrom

3) This procedure first seemed to execute correctly. 73 subsets were moved from
the CD-ROM to disk.

4) Then a "Merge" phase followed, which in most cases completed successfully,
but in a few cases told me to inspect files in /var/adm/smlogs after the update
for a manual merge. I don't think this is something to worry about.

5) Then something that worries me. Three times a message is displayed about the
usage of mv. I don't remember the exact message, because it scrolled from the
screen before I could write it down. Each message has three lines starting with
something like
usage: mv [-i]
This could indicate that some files were not placed correctly.

6) Then the system automatically reboots with a new generic system. There is
another "merge" phase.

7) The procedure then starts to build a new system. Because of the -i option of
installupdate it asks for subsystems to include and I selected the cdfs file
system.

8) The system build seems to complete successfully and the system attempts to
reboot with this new system.

9) This boot tells that it successfully switched to the OSF PALcode and then
displays the following message:
Internal limit reached in OSF_BOOT: Out of space for pass2 segments
and it returns to the >>> prompt.

I restored the original 3.2C system from a backup tape and tried it again,
starting from step 2, this time not using the -i option of installupdate, with
the same result.

I would like to know whether there is a way to repair the new 4.0 system so
that it will boot again, or whether there is another path from 3.2C to 4.0D
that has more chance to succeed.

Any other suggestions are welcome, too.

