In article <6s1g8p$lk0$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, heitbaum@my-dejanews.com writes:
>I am having the following problem with a multithreaded application compiled
>with GNU C++ under DU4.0d. I have read the FAQ page, and know what the
>problem is but does any one have a suggestion on how to track this down.
>
>Unaligned access pid=7579 <darxStoreServer> va=0x3ff800dd634 pc=0x120022a64
>ra=0x120022a40 inst=0x8c220000

Unlike most UNIX I've used, on DU you can request a map from ld when you
build the application, that will tell you which routine contains the
instruction at 0x120022a64.  Some compilers may help you find what
instruction in that routine is causing the problem and what line of code
is related to that instruction.  Subtract the start address of the
routine from the pc value to determine how far into the routine you
are, then see if there's a compiler listing file capability, or an
intermediate (.s) file which might help you find the instruction at that
offset.

More like other UNIX, you can probably use dbg or ladebug to read the
core file and find the instruction.  You may have trouble relating this
to a line of code if the application was built without -g.

