In article <6qodbi$mrs$1@fddinewz.oit.unc.edu>,
Eric Frey <frey@hawk.bme.unc.edu> wrote:
> We have versions of several programs (e.g., pine and tin) compiled under
> some flavor of DUX 3.2. When we try to run these binaries under
> 4.0d they give messages like:
> ---
> 28054:/usr/local/bin/pine: /sbin/loader: Fatal Error: object
> libcurses.so from liblist in /usr/local/bin/pine has version "osf.1",
> which does not match the found object: /usr/shlib/libcurses.so (with
> version "xpg4")
> ---
> It sounds like we are missing the old version of the shared library. Is there
> a way that I can copy this from our older systems. I'd like to avoid having
> several binaries around, one for each  version of DUX. Thanks.

You can take the option others offered by using the environment variable to
cause the loader to ignore library versioning.  This should work in a pinch,
but I would avoid it.

You could load the appropriate 4.0d fileset that contains the old library.
I believe the fileset is OSFOBSOLETE425.  This is probably the best solution,
but since you're trying to avoid differences between machines it will introduce
a new difference -- 4.0d machines will have that fileset loaded while earlier
machines may not.  Of course, using 3.x and 4.x introduces a bunch of
differences anyway, so you can probably live with this.

You could just find /usr/shlib/libcurses.so from a 4.0 - 4.0c box, and install
it as

	/usr/shlib/osf.1/libcurses.so

on the 4.0d box.  This is essentially what loading the OSFOBSOLETE425 fileset
does anyway, but you get other stuff with the OSFOBSOLETE425 fileset.

Note that the change in libcurses between 3.x and 4.x was mentioned in the
release notes, and the fact that the compatibility library was not installed
by default at 4.0d is also mentioned in the 4.0d release notes.

