AdvFS is getting better, and works well for a lot of purposes, but it's
still one of my biggest "worries" about Digital Unix.  I've actually had to
use the "new" salvage utility to salvage three multi-fileset file domains,
and it worked like a charm, but I still don't trust the tools.  That's very
bad, especially when you're not completely confidant in the filesystem either.

As far as AdvFS being "the one" reason to use Digital Unix, I can think of
lots of things I like about Digital Unix better than AdvFS.

I do know that some large sites are using AdvFS for news spools, but I don't
know how well it's going for them.

Considering I'm a really big proponent of Digital Unix, my comments should
probably be viewed as pretty stern criticism of AdvFS.  We've had generally
good luck with it, and we do use it widely (including root filesystems and
/var/spool/mail on some busy machines), but that's not to say that I'm not
nervous about it.

If you're going to use AdvFS on a machine, it's probably a good idea to
use *only* AdvFS, so you can avoid having both an AdvFS cache and a big
UBC.

