Bruce Bowler wrote:
> 
> I'm running V3.0 of OSF/1 (I know it's old, but I'm stuck there).  I
> just tried to turn on enhanced security using secsetup and everything
> seemed to go fine except that after the reboot, no one could log in.
> Booting to single user and rerunning secsetup selecting basic "fixed it"
> so that people could log in again.  Looking at the logs shows nothing
> "unusual".

That is the problem with all 3.xxx versions. It happened to me 8 out of 10
times. The cure is:

1. Since even root can't log in, reset, or even better, halt your machine.

2. Boot to single user, "boot -flags s"

3. Change password for root, "passwd"

4. Go to run-level-3.

5. Log in as root and figure out what to do next. I've always done secsetup
first.

There are some commands that could help, like "convauth".

This bug was noted in DU 4.0, since the procedure asks you to change root
password BEFORE rebooting or "you won't be able to log in after...".

Nix.





