> > Also, speaking of firmware updates, this box only has one very old SCSI
> > drive that I'd like to replace. How picky is DUNIX going to be about this?
> > Will it cause me problems not to use a DEC drive?

DEC and Digital Unix are friendly with non-DEC hard drives, so what the drive
reports for manufacturer/model/revision when probed shouldn't make any
difference to Digital Unix.

> Any SCSI drive should function.  That's what SCSI is all about.

I'm guessing that's a bit generous, but perhaps you know something I
don't. Are you suggesting that the original poster shouldn't have any problems
using a wide SCSI drive on the machine, even if the onboard SCSI controller
is narrow?  I think that's unlikely.

Although I certainly could be wrong, I would suggest that the original poster
should determine what type of onboard SCSI the machine has (most likely
Fast, Narrow, Single-Ended, aka FNSE, but it could be something else), and
buy a drive of that type.

Tim
