> Problems due to incorrect SMP algorithms are very difficult to debug.  It is
> much easier to do the research and get it right the first time....

From this discusion I made following concept. Following structures 
are in the shared memory.

Proc 1: spinlock, some data, current_accessed_item
Proc 2: 


ItemTable: 
  Item 1: index_to_the_real_item
  Item 2: ..


RealItem:
  Item 1: (Some data depending on the item)
  Item 2: ...


So now the proc 1 tries to access Item 1:
- lock its spinlock
- read real address of item1 from ItemTable
- set the current_accessed_item to the found address
- unlock its spinlock
- read the data

If a writer (there is only one at a time) want to 
change item 1 it do following:
- lock global writer spinlock
- alloc and set a new item in RealItem,
- queue the old address of item 1 in a cleanup-queue.
- write new address into item 1 in ItemTable
- unlock global writer spinlock.

Some server do following:
- lock gloabal writer spinlock
- get an item address out of cleanup-queue
- unlock gloabal writer spinlock.
- check if someone accesses this item currently.
  if yes put it again in the queue
  if no 
  	lock gloabal writer spinlock
  	mark the item as reusable
	unlock gloabal writer spinlock.
- continue

The critical part is writing the current_accessed_item
and the index_to_the_real_item. It must be guaranteed that
after the unlock of the proc spinlock current_accessed_item
has the correct value for any process on any processor.
(How do I handle that?)

For the index_to_the_real_item it is needed that the reader
will allways get a valid (which may be old) address. I think
that ATOMIC_EXCHANGE... can be used by the writer. 
(with or without _MB?)

Bye,

