Look, if I'm just incrementing a variable (the problem being described
by the person in question) there's no reason to even *hint* at the
possibility of a context switch forced by the thread library during
that incremenet.

E.g., if I'm going

	pthread_mutex_lock(foo);
	counter++;
	pthread_mutex_unlock(foo);

it will take more time to get into/out of the O/S than to simply way.

Many performance oriented programmers would know this and exercise
proper judgement about when to use a spinlock. 

However, without representative & provided "spinlock" code, even
expert programmers will screw up the WB's, MB's and the like needed
for correctness.

We shouldn't assume all programmers don't know how to write concurrent code.

-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



