Let's break things down into two cases:  Shared-read/exclusive-write data
who correctness is (case 1) not critical or (case 2) critical.

Case 1:  An application can elect not to put the data under a mutex.
All algorithms that use that data as input must be prepared to deal with
incorrect or inconsistent values in the structure.  Candidates for this
class are:  performance counters, profiling code, debug flags.  Other
application data can fall into this class keeping the incorrectness/
inconsistency aspect in mind and communicating it to those poor bastards
who have to do maintenance on the thing once you've gone on to greener
pastures.

Case 2:  Since I write device drivers and similar kernel code, almost
everything I do falls into this area.  I'll discuss this in an application
space, which is slightly different from kernel, where there are roughly
four approaches:

Case 2.a:  Do nothing.  This will get you into trouble.  It's hard to
debug and your co-workers will hate you.  You will be forced to write
Visual Basic faux-programs for the rest of your life.

Case 2.b:  System mutex.  In the case of Digital Unix, use pthreads,
mach thread control, or TIS services to implement a classic mutex 
around the data.  In the case of pthreads, the system services even
guarantee data ordering in an SMP system between system calls.  That
is, all the requirements of section 5.6 of the Alpha SRM are satisified
with respect to cpu/cpu operations.  Device/cpu ordering is a separate
issue.

Case 2.c:  System mutex with syscall avoidance.  The idea is that syscalls
are expensive (a relative measure as I'll detail in a moment) so avoiding
them is A Good Thing.  It is achieved by using an interlocked counter or
similar mechanism to detect contention for a mutex and to only make 
mutex calls when contention arises.  I've offered the BeOS' Benaphores
up as an example of this and they are generally a win for infrequent
locking.  However, there can be major performance hits due to scheduler
activity.  See the Be Newsletters for details.

Case 2.d:  User-mode spinlocks.  In a way, this is a return to DOS programming.
The idea is that the cost of a thread simply spinning on an smp-safe flag
will be less than the cost of entering and exiting a pair of system services.
There a number of reasons why this may not be so which I'll go into in a 
moment.  First, I'll discuss implementation.  An smp-safe spinlock cannot
be constructed from STx, LDx, and MB.  It must be built from LDx_L and
STx_C instructions.  These are the load locked/store conditionals that
use the architecture- and implementation-defined interlocked access protocols
to guarantee that multiple cpus see identical versions of longwords or
quadwords.  From an old version of the SRM, here's a spin-lock algorithm
(may contain errors):

	stq_c_loop:
	spin_loop:
		LDQ_L	r1, lock_variable
		BLBS	r1, already_set
		OR	r1, #1, r2
		STQ_C	r2, lock_variable
		BEQ	r2, stq_c_fail
		MB
	; Begin critical section
		<Your code deleted>
	; End critical section
		MB
		STQ	r31, lock_variable	; clear lock
	; Return to application code

	; Forward branching failure targets
	already_set:
	; Sleep, check for too many iterations, or do nothing
		BR	spin_loop
	stq_c_fail:
	; Check for too many interations.
		BR	stq_c_loop


Now let's consider the merits:

.  Cost of syscall versus cost of spinlock contention.  The time to execute
a trivial syscall (trap, prepare return value, dismiss trap) on an EV5-class
alpha can be as low as 500 nS (nano).  Obviously, a real syscall is going to
take more time.  It's been some time since I've benchmarked it but a heavy-
weight syscall using the ioctl interface, a sleep, an interrupt in an
ISR routine to do a wakeup was under 10 uS and 6 uS comes to mind.  A mutex
acquire/release pair could be as fast as 1 uS for no contention, and on
the order of 10 uS (plus sleep time) with contention.  The user-mode spinlock
widens the spread.  First, with no contention, you still need a correct
implementation of the spinlock and that will eat a few cycles (see algorithm)
but is still faster than a syscall.  On a single processor, with, say,
a 10,000 uS scheduling quantum, encountering contention, the losing thread
is in a cpu-spin and will have to exhaust its quantum before the lock-owning
thread can proceed and release the lock.  In the best case, the lock-owning
thread is scheduled, clears the lock, and *immediately* sleeps allowing
the first thread to execute and do useful work.  In this case, the statistical
expected value for lock acquisition is 5,000 uS.  I.e. lower bound is half
the scheduling quantum.  There are worse cases:  The lock-owning thread
is cpu-bound and continues to consume a full quantum every time it is
scheduled.  Expected value for lock acquisition:  15,000 uS or 1.5 times
the scheduling quantum.  Worse still:  lock-owning thread consumes a quantum
and re-acquires lock just before losing quantum.  Expected value for lock
acquisition:  >1.5 times the scheduling quantum.  Now we have:

T.exp.mut	Expected time to execute real mutex code
T.exp.spin	Expected time to execute user-mode spinlock code
T.exp.unlock	Expected time to unlock mutex under contention
T.spin.nocont	Expected time to acquire spinlock with no contention
P.mut.cont	Probability of encountering contention with real mutex code
P.spin.cont	Probability of encountering contention with user-mode spinlock
P.best		Probability of best-case spinlock contention resolution
P.worse		Probability of worse-case spinlock contention resolution
P.worst		Probability of worst-case spinlock contention resolution

	T.exp.mut = (P.mut.cont * (10uS + T.exp.unlock))
		  + ((1 - P.mut.cont) * 1uS)

	T.exp.spin = (P.spin.cont * ((P.best * 5,000 uS)
				    +(P.worse * 15,000 uS)
				    +(P.worst * 20,000 uS)))
		     + ((1 - P.spin.cont) * T.spin.nocont)

	1 ~ P.best + P.worse + P.worst

We can make some assumptions:

	P.best should be near 1.
	T.spin.nocont is on the order of 10's of nS unless some of the
		situations below arise.
	T.exp.unlock is dependent upon the application's algorithms but it
		also represents useful work as opposed to the time
		consumed under the P.spin.cont term which is dead CPU.
		How you weight it is a designer's decision.

The goal is to keep T.exp.spin well under (i.e. the benefit outweighs 
the effort of supporting and debugging home-grown spinlock code on
every supported system) T.exp.mut.  Obviously, there are several 
independent variables but a few will likely dominate.  If P.spin.cont is
much greater than 1/5000, its term dominates T.exp.spin and bounds
that latter to a minimum of 1uS which is the lower bound for T.exp.mut
regardless of P.mut.cont.  What do you need to do?  Profile your application
so that you can estimate P.spin.cont.  Certain application decisions
will affect this value.  If the spinlock is coarse-grained, i.e. a few
locks cover all data structures, the probability goes up.  As the time
required to execute the code in the critical section goes up, this
probability goes up.  If the spinlocks are fine-grained, the probability
goes down *but* T.spin.nocont starts to increase exponentially.  That
is because the system bus protocol for interlocked operations typically
has very large granularity.

.  On an SMP system, the times under the P.spin.cont term should be
smaller.  A thread has the potential to get running on one processor and
clear the lock allowing a blocked thread on the other system to run (which
is why spinlocks work well for kernel mode code which first assumes priority
ordering under spl level).  However, with more than two threads, or more
specifically, with too many more threads than cpus, P.spin.cont goes
back up.  And with too many cpus, T.spin.nocont goes up (this represents
the asymptotic limit of smp systems).

.  The user-mode spinlocks are subject to some of the problems of 2.c.
See lit. for details.

.  The particular times in the above are not definitive.  I haven't 
benchmarked the pthread, mach thread, or tis services.  Only the kernel-mode
sleep mechanisms, interrupt dispatching, and trap handling.  The numbers
I present are certainly possible but what is actually achieved in the
libraries needs competent benchmarking.

Finally, if you go down the SMP route, make *certain* you develop and
test on an SMP system.  You can violate all the data ordering requirements
on a single processor system and everything will work correctly as a
single cpu sees only one consistent view of the system.  Put that code
on an smp machine and it will very quickly fall apart in obvious and
not-so-obvious ways.

> However, without representative & provided "spinlock" code, even
> expert programmers will screw up the WB's, MB's and the like needed
> for correctness.

Representative and provided spinlock code is in the Alpha SRM.  If you are
attempting to implement SMP algorithms on Alpha without this book, or the
corresponding book on other architectures, you're not doing your
employers good service.

> We shouldn't assume all programmers don't know how to write concurrent code.

That's a nice sentiment but it doesn't stand up to scutiny.  It's a
better than one sigma programmer who can use explicit concurrent programming
services correctly.  (I present the Modula-3 project's motivation as proof.)
Those who can actually implement the services correctly are out at
three sigma, I'd say.

