According to representatives of DEC/Compaq, who have been trying to
avoid fixing some performance problems in their disk strategy code
(affects UFS local, local being local or on a HSZ system and remote
UFS filesystems accessed via NFS), most ISPs running DEC OSF are
using AdvFS and we should be using Advfs instead of UFS for all of
our filesystems, and in particular, our news spools and root
filesystems.

Based on written statements they sent us, essentially nobody
(at least in DEC/Compaqs opinion) still uses UFS, so they don't see any
urgent (now 18 months on this ticket) reason to address these
problems, and are talking about another six months (after Steele,
aka OSF 5) before even thinking about looking at scheduling work
on possibly addressing it.   Obviously, we aren't too happy about
that, and are thumbing through those Sun catalogs we get in the mail.

In a nutshell, the bug is some code that DEC deleted from the standard
UNIX disk strategy functions so that when UBC is completely filled
with dirty buffers, and the system finally starts writing them out,
no new reads are allowed from any block until every single one of
those buffers are written, causing the system to stall.  All disk
I/O requests are now essentially a simple FIFO, no read prioritization,
no block-rewrite consolidation, no elevator sorting.  The more main
memory, the longer the stall becomes, and in our 512Meg and 1GB
systems, other apps can hang for as long as two minutes, while the
CPUs are essentially 100% idle.  When eventyally the UBC is completely
written, other processes wake up and things move along until the busy
application manages to fill UBC and the pausing cycle repeats.  

Even for something as small as a 4Meg passwd file, mkpasswd can
bring a three processor 4/275MHz 1GB main memory Alpha system to
a screaming halt every minute or so for the five to ten minutes
mkpasswd ends up taking to run.  The old UUCP pathalias can also
make this monster of a computer unusable for everybody else for
an even longer period of time (more data).

This was also demonstrated to DEC as a Denial-of-Service attack an
unpriviledged shell user could launch against the system.  That
didn't move things along either.

The bug is easy to reproduce, just rebuild a large (say 20,000 or more
entries) passwd file database, or large mail alias database, or
rebuild a INN history database (makehistory or expire will do if you
set max datasize smaller than the history.pag size so it has to be
built on disk for the most impressive demonstration - also fails
