or two every now and then over the run-life of the other
application.   When ps will actually run, you will see the killer
application is perpetually in U state.   In extreme cases, telnet
sessions character echo will stop working during the pauses.

The bug was originally reported in February of 1997, and exists in all
OSF 4.x versions (Ultrix works fine!), and now DEC/Compaq is basically
saying, switch to Advfs or live with the bug.  "Everyone else is
using Advfs" is what we are being told and "it works much better,
honest!"  But, They don't really guarantee that a migration to Advfs
will solve this problem and won't cause other performance problems
to surface.   We are suspicious since remote UFS systems accessed via
NFS show the same symptoms as UFS filesystems on local drives.

So, all I am asking here is: does everybody now use Advfs and only Advfs?
Anyone who is still using UFS for big disk applications, like USENET
News?  If so, please speak up.   Thanks for your time.


