The last time I checked, the lowest-end current card that will do 24 bits is
the 4D20.  Digital has dropped prices quite a bit in the last two months, but
the 4D20 still costs about $1,500.

Poor support for commodity video and audio on desktop systems has long been
a favorite complaint for me about Digital Unix.  Having to pay $1500 to get
24 bit video is IMHO absurd.  Then there's the fact that Multimedia Services
doesn't support any common (can you say "SoundBlaster") audio cards.  I'm a
big fan of Digital Unix, but support for commodity audio and video hardware
does not seem to be a priority, which is really unfortunate.  I've seen the
"How do I get 24 bit video without selling my children" question asked many
times, and the answer is always "you can't unless you write your own driver".

