It's a wee bit hard to tell what might be going on without a better
look.
__start is basically the jump off routine invoked by the os (at least
I think that's right).  You are wondering if this is caused by trying to

debug a core file from a more recent version of the os.  Are you indeed
doing this?  It could be that __start is in libc and if you have
different
shared libraries on your system then the one that generated the core
file,
then you may well be getting some strange error message like this that
does not correspond to what actually happened.  But this is just a
guess.  Any info you could give as to where the core file came from
and how you are attempting to debug it could help.  By the way, if
this is a core from another system, you can sometimes get a
bit more info by copying the shared libraries from the system that
produced the core, putting them in a separate directory, and
then setting the environment variable LD_LIBRARY_PATH to
point to this directory.  No guarantee that this will work, and
your normal commands will attempt to use this path, so normal commands
like ls have to be invoked as /sbin/ls.

PeterT




